I want you to create a plan that you can later follow and implement, for a new
programming project:

I have a personal collection of photos, roughly 300 GB of mostly JPEG photos,
stored on an Internet connected server running Linux. The photos are organized
in a standard hierarchical directory structure, the top level is mostly by year
but doesn't have to (e.g., I'll have an "ancient" subdirectory), and lower levels
are often individual months, sometimes topical directories, and sometimes a hierarchy
of several layers.

I want to run a Web site similar to the UI of Google Photos on this server: I want my
family members to have individual accounts that I give them and be able to log in,
and then see these photos (unauthorized people will not be able to see anything).
The normal ways to browse should be a very speedy UI of thumbnails of the photos in
a directory, and be able to go into a subdirectory or back to parent directory, as
well as the ability to a single-photo view view and see larger photos - and finally
the ability to download individual original-sized photos.

The UI should be nicely viewable on any modern computer running Firefox or Chrome,
as well as a browser on phone, and be convenient, pretty and responsive.

The server is weak in CPU, so it should prepare things in advance: If we want to
show small thumbnails or screen-size images we should prepare them in advance,
and also compress them efficiently to use up little disk and bandwidth, and then
viewing the thumbnails et al. should be very responsive. We could also create
static HTML for a lot of the content (of course, the authentication part will need
to be dynamic). Although I do have enough space for the 300 GB of photos, I don't
have huge amounts of space to spare, so the implementation should also be careful
about disk space use (the smaller photos are fine, even the large versions should
be an order of magnitude smaller than the originals).

The server is running Linux and the implementation needs to use only free software,
and if possible things that will not require very large or complex installations.
If I had to choose a programming language I like most, I prefer Python, but I'm
open to be convinced to choose other languages.

You can use existing libraries, databases, etc., but again I would prefer to
not be too complex, prefer libraries that typical Linux installations already have
or can easily get, and things I will be able to understand later.

Some more ideas I had:

* When I upload new photos and new subdirectories to the photo directory, I want
  to be able to run a program which will rescan the photos, figure out what changed
  (added or deleted) and create/delete the thumbnails, files, databases, or whatever
  needs to be changed. This process should be relatively efficient - I won't do it
  often but I also don't want it to take many hours for 300 GB of photos.

* Each subdirectory (including the top-level subirectory) will have a representative
  photo. Initially it will be chosen randomly (perhaps just the first photo), but
  I want to be able to choose a different one.

* To choosing the representative photo of a directory, or other types of configuration
  or metadata, I don't want to write this information into a database - I want to be
  able to write it ordinary files inside the directory, in some configuration format.
  For example, I want to be able to set the "thumbnail" (the representative photo
  for the directory), the "title" of the directory, etc.

* I will run the same software both on my home Linux machine and on the (weak) server,
  which reiterates the fact I want it to be relatively easy to install the prerequisite
  packages. I'm a Linux expert - I won't have problems installing things - but I don't
  want to install a bazillion things.

* I want to have an option to support Google accounts. For example, I'll say that
  "somebody@gmail.com" can use the site, and anyone that Google claims authenticated
  as somebody@gmail.com will be able to see it.

* I want to be able to have a URLs that points to the top album but also URLs that
  point to individual subdirectories.

* I want to be able to have an option (again, configurable in the configuration
  file stored in each subdirectory) to allow only specific authenticated users to
  look into a certain directory (and its subdirectories).
  
